-- Library coverage for the relationship types nobody else serves. The free
-- library had nothing specific to siblings, coworkers or mentors, and the
-- parent–teen piece was premium only. Four free, evidence-rated reads that
-- the public /for pages and the hub can point to. Idempotent: slugs are
-- unique and existing rows are left alone.

insert into education_articles (slug,title,summary,body,category,relationship_type,framework,evidence_rating,is_premium) values
('adult-siblings-meeting-again',
 'Adult siblings: meeting again as grown-ups',
 'Siblings share a childhood and often not much else as adults. The roles you were handed at eight still steer the conversation at forty, unless you name them.',
 E'Sibling relationships are usually the longest of our lives, and the least tended. Research on adult siblings finds that closeness tends to dip in early adulthood, when careers and partners take centre stage, and often recovers in midlife, especially around a parent''s illness or death, when siblings are thrown back together.\n\nTwo patterns do most of the damage in between:\n\n- **Frozen roles.** Family-systems research describes how roles (the responsible one, the baby, the peacemaker, the difficult one) get assigned early and stick. Adults keep reacting to the eight-year-old version of each other. Naming the role out loud ("I know I''m still the bossy one to you") loosens it more than any amount of being different.\n- **Perceived favouritism.** Studies of adult siblings consistently link remembered parental favouritism to lower sibling closeness decades later, whether or not the favouritism was real. It rarely gets said, so it never gets corrected.\n\nWhat helps, from the research on sibling closeness:\n\n- **Make contact a ritual, not an obligation.** A standing monthly call beats guilt-driven sporadic ones. Frequency predicts closeness more than the content of the contact.\n- **Trade the family story for your own.** Ask about their life now, not the shared past. Siblings often know each other''s childhood in detail and each other''s present barely at all.\n- **Say the old thing once, kindly.** Most sibling grudges are small, old and unspoken. One honest sentence and one non-defensive hearing is usually enough; relitigating is not.\n- **Plan for the parents before the crisis.** Caregiving is where sibling relationships most often break. Deciding early how care and decisions will be shared protects the relationship as much as the parent.\n\nThe evidence here is mostly survey-based and correlational, so treat it as a map of common traps rather than a formula.',
 'family','sibling','family_systems',3,false),

('two-coworkers-psychological-safety',
 'Psychological safety between two coworkers',
 'The research on team safety scales down to two people: can each of you say the risky thing without paying for it? That one question predicts most of how well you work together.',
 E'Psychological safety is the shared belief that it is safe to take interpersonal risks: to ask the naive question, flag the mistake, disagree with the plan. In Amy Edmondson''s team studies and in Google''s later internal research, it was the strongest predictor of team effectiveness. Between two people who work closely, it is simpler to build and easier to break.\n\nThe mechanics are small and daily:\n\n- **Respond to the first risk well.** The first time one of you admits a mistake or pushes back, the other person''s reaction sets the rule for the next year. Curiosity ("what did you see?") builds safety; a sigh or a quick fix lowers it.\n- **Make your own fallibility visible.** Saying "I may have missed something, tell me" is not weakness; it is the cheapest invitation to candour there is.\n- **Separate the person from the error.** Blameless debriefs ("what happened, what would we change") are the single most effective habit in the research. Blame drives the next mistake underground.\n- **Be clear about ownership.** Unclear roles are a quiet safety killer: when nobody knows whose call it is, raising a concern feels like an accusation.\n- **Credit in public, correct in private.** Specific, public recognition is the cheapest trust-builder a workplace has.\n\nWhat safety is not: being nice, agreeing, or lowering the bar. The safest pairs in the research also had the highest standards. Safety is what lets the standard be discussed.\n\nEvidence: strong for teams (multiple field studies and meta-analyses), applied here to pairs by extension rather than tested directly.',
 'work','coworker','psychological_safety',4,false),

('mentoring-that-actually-works',
 'Mentoring that actually works',
 'Most mentoring fizzles for lack of structure, not goodwill. The relationships that help have a clear purpose, regular contact, honest feedback and a network behind them.',
 E'Decades of mentoring research, in workplaces and in youth programmes, agree on something uncomfortable: a mentoring relationship that drifts or ends early can leave the mentee worse off than no mentor at all. The relationships that help share a few features.\n\n- **A named purpose.** "Help me get promoted", "help me decide whether to leave", "help me stop avoiding hard conversations". Vague mentoring produces pleasant chats and no change. Revisit the purpose every few months; it moves.\n- **Regular, predictable contact.** Frequency and consistency predict outcomes better than the mentor''s seniority. A reliable monthly hour beats an occasional famous name.\n- **Feedback the mentee can use.** Mentors tend to under-deliver critical feedback for fear of discouraging. Mentees consistently say they wanted more of it. Ask how they want it (blunt, framed, written) and then give it.\n- **The hard question.** The most valuable moments in mentoring come when the mentee asks what they were afraid to ask. Mentors can make that cheaper by saying, early, what questions are welcome.\n- **A network, not a hero.** Developmental-network research finds people grow fastest with several developers (a sponsor, a peer, a skills coach, a mentor) rather than one all-purpose guide. A good mentor opens doors to the others.\n- **It goes both ways.** Reverse mentoring is real: mentors report learning, fresh perspective and renewed engagement. Mentees who are asked for their view show up more honestly.\n\nEvidence: consistent across workplace and youth mentoring studies; effect sizes are modest on average and strongly dependent on the relationship''s quality and length.',
 'work','mentor','developmental_network',4,false),

('teens-autonomy-not-surveillance',
 'Teens, trust and the case against surveillance',
 'Monitoring apps promise safety; the research on adolescent development says connection does the job better. Teens who feel their autonomy is respected tell their parents more, not less.',
 E'Parents of teenagers are sold two options: watch everything, or hope for the best. The developmental research points to a third that works better than either.\n\n**What the research says**\n\n- Adolescents disclose more to parents they experience as warm and autonomy-supportive, and less to parents who rely on control and monitoring. Parental knowledge of a teen''s life comes mostly from the teen choosing to tell, not from surveillance (Kerr and Stattin''s work on monitoring is the classic finding here).\n- Self-determination theory describes three needs that drive healthy motivation at every age: autonomy, competence and relatedness. Teens whose autonomy is supported show better adjustment, less secrecy and fewer risky behaviours, not more.\n- Covert monitoring, when discovered, is experienced as a breach and tends to produce exactly the secrecy it was meant to prevent.\n\n**What autonomy support looks like in practice**\n\n- **Explain, don''t just rule.** Rules with reasons are followed more and resented less than rules by decree.\n- **Hand over decisions on purpose.** Choose a real decision, give it fully, and let the outcome teach. Trust that is never extended is never learned.\n- **Ask with curiosity, not inspection.** "What was the best part?" opens a door; "where exactly were you?" closes it.\n- **React to disclosures well.** The first time a teen tells you something hard, your reaction decides whether there is a second time. Validate the feeling before anything else; this is the core of emotion coaching.\n- **Keep the safety floor explicit.** Autonomy support is not permissiveness. Name the few non-negotiables (safety, honesty about where you are when it matters) and hold them warmly.\n\nEvidence: strong and consistent across longitudinal studies of adolescents; effect sizes are moderate. None of this replaces professional help if a teen is in danger.',
 'parenting','parent_child','autonomy_support',4,false)
